    "explanation": "本題的關鍵在於內函數的導數。學生常誤寫為 (2x+1)⁶ ÷ 6 + C，漏了除以 2。判斷方法很直接：把答案微分回去，若得不到原式即知漏了步驟。\n\n【Casio fx-50FH II / 3650P 輔助】\n不定積分無法由計數機直接求得，但【定積分】可以，用以驗算答案：\n在 COMP 模式輸入　∫( (2X+1)^5 , 0 , 1 )\n與自己求得的原函數代入上下限相減對比。若兩者相同，答案正確。\n以本題為例：∫₀¹ (2x+1)⁵ dx = [(2x+1)⁶ ÷ 12]₀¹ = (729 − 1) ÷ 12 = 728 ÷ 12 ≈ 60.667\n計數機應同樣得出約 60.667。若你漏了除以 2，會得 121.33，即刻看得出差了一倍。\n⚠️ 定積分運算較慢，須耐心等候；範圍宜取簡單數值以便手算對照。",
    "referenceAnswer": "解法（以代換法）：\n設 u = 2x + 1\n則 du/dx = 2，即 du = 2 dx，故 dx = du ÷ 2\n\n∫ (2x + 1)⁵ dx = ∫ u⁵ × (du ÷ 2)\n　= (1 ÷ 2) ∫ u⁵ du\n　= (1 ÷ 2) × (u⁶ ÷ 6) + C\n　= u⁶ ÷ 12 + C\n　= (2x + 1)⁶ ÷ 12 + C\n\n何以不可直接略去其餘步驟：\n「指數加一，除以新指數」這條規則來自冪法則 ∫ xⁿ dx = x^(n+1) ÷ (n+1) + C，而該規則的變數必須是 x【本身】，不可以是 x 的函數。\n此處被提升次方的是 (2x + 1)，一個複合結構。若直接寫成 (2x+1)⁶ ÷ 6 + C，可以微分驗證其錯誤：\n\nd/dx [(2x+1)⁶ ÷ 6] = (1 ÷ 6) × 6(2x+1)⁵ × 2　【鏈式法則，勿忘內函數導數 2】\n　= 2(2x + 1)⁵\n\n所得為原式的【兩倍】，故該答案錯誤。要抵銷這個多出來的因子 2，原函數須再除以 2，即 (2x+1)⁶ ÷ 12。\n\n驗算正確答案：\nd/dx [(2x+1)⁶ ÷ 12] = (1 ÷ 12) × 6(2x+1)⁵ × 2 = (12 ÷ 12)(2x+1)⁵ = (2x+1)⁵ ✓\n\n概念要點：\n積分是微分的逆運算，而微分複合函數時鏈式法則會【乘上】內函數的導數。故積分時必須【除以】它，才能抵銷。\n本題內函數為線性（2x + 1），其導數為常數 2，所以「除以 2」這一步簡單。\n若內函數為非線性（例如 (x² + 1)⁵），則其導數 2x 並非常數，不能簡單地除出來 —— 此時必須有 x 這個因子出現在被積函數之中，代換才行得通。這正是為何 ∫ x(x²+1)⁵ dx 可解而 ∫ (x²+1)⁵ dx 不能以同樣方法處理。\n\n通式（值得記住）：\n∫ (ax + b)ⁿ dx = (ax + b)^(n+1) ÷ [a(n + 1)] + C　（n ≠ −1）",
    "marks": 1,
    "referenceAnswerEn": "Solution by substitution: let u = 2x + 1, so du/dx = 2, du = 2 dx and dx = du/2.\nThe integral becomes (1/2) times the integral of u^5 du = (1/2)(u^6 / 6) + C = u^6 / 12 + C = (2x + 1)^6 / 12 + C.\n\nWhy the shortcut fails: the rule of raising the exponent and dividing comes from the power rule, whose variable must be x itself rather than a function of x. Here the base is (2x + 1), a composite.\nWriting (2x+1)^6 / 6 + C can be shown wrong by differentiating: d/dx[(2x+1)^6 / 6] = (1/6) x 6(2x+1)^5 x 2 = 2(2x+1)^5, by the chain rule, which is twice the original. To cancel the extra factor of 2 the antiderivative must be divided by 2, giving (2x+1)^6 / 12.\nChecking the correct answer: d/dx[(2x+1)^6 / 12] = (1/12) x 6(2x+1)^5 x 2 = (2x+1)^5.\n\nThe concept: integration reverses differentiation, and differentiating a composite multiplies by the derivative of the inner function, so integration must divide by it.\nHere the inner function is linear and its derivative is the constant 2, so dividing is straightforward. Where the inner function is non-linear, as in (x^2 + 1)^5, its derivative 2x is not constant and cannot simply be divided out; a factor of x must already be present in the integrand for the substitution to work. This is why the integral of x(x^2+1)^5 is tractable while that of (x^2+1)^5 is not by the same method.\n\nGeneral form worth remembering: the integral of (ax + b)^n dx is (ax + b)^(n+1) / [a(n + 1)] + C, for n not equal to -1.",
